Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_skills", "contents": [{"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Listening", "type": "skill"}]}]}], "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}]}], "type": "sena", "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Advanced Concepts on Object Oriented Programming ", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 3, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.1", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear and use when talking about OOP.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Talk about principles, concepts, characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob03re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "The Future With Will, Going to, the Present Continuous and the Present Simple", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "There are many different ways to talk about future plans or events.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<p><strong>WILL </strong></p> <p>You use <strong>will</strong> to talk about future plans as long as some conditions are met. The use of <strong>will</strong> can be used to talk about an opinion or prediction about the future, a spontaneous decision taken at the moment, or a promise. The use of <strong>will</strong> goes along with expressions like <i>probably, maybe, perhaps, </i>among others.</p> <p>Use the following structure:</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject + will + verb (base form) + complement.</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>For example:</p> <p><i>She<strong> will probably</strong> <strong>invest</strong> in our company next year. </i></p> <p><i>Gabriel and Mark just made their decision; they <b>will buy</b> our software. </i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>GOING TO </strong></p> <p>You use <strong>going to</strong> to talk about future plans depending on the situation; when you express a prediction based on the present time or to talk about a plan you have already made the decision to do. Remember to use the verb <strong>to be</strong> right before the expression <strong>going to</strong>. Take a look at the structure we use:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;Subject + am/is/are + going to + verb (base form) + complement</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>For example:</p> <p><i>I <strong>am going to</strong> try that new software today. &nbsp;</i></p> <p><i>The meeting<strong> is going to</strong> include the intervention of Mr. Gonzalez, a special engineer of the committee.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>PRESENT CONTINUOUS </strong></p> <p>You can use the <strong>present continuous</strong> tense to talk about plans in the future; however, the plan you are referring to must be already planned or prepared in some degree. It is necessary to include a time expression that refers to the future. The structure we use is the following:</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject + am/is/are + action verb in gerund (-ing) + complement</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>For example:</p> <p><i>We<strong> are meeting</strong> with the new staff of the tech department tomorrow <strong>at 7 o&rsquo;clock</strong>. &nbsp;</i></p> <p><i>The company<strong> is launching </strong>the new product <strong>next summer in July</strong>.</i></p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>PRESENT SIMPLE</strong></p> <p>You can use the <strong>present simple</strong> to talk about situations that are scheduled or already arranged. Apart from using the <strong>present simple</strong> tense, the only requirement in here is to explicitly add the future time phrase that mentions when the situation or event is going to take place.</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>For example:</p> <p><i>The investors arrive <strong>tonight at 8 pm</strong>. </i></p> <p><i>The meeting finishes <strong>at 6.30 in the afternoon.</strong></i></p>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I will talk today about OOP.   ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You're going to have to know about all aspects related to this topic if you want to become an outstanding programmer. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Anyway, don't forget that I arrive tonight at your city; I'm speaking at the programmers' convention tomorrow at 6! ", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "outstanding ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "successful; one of the best", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: You're going to have to know about all aspects related to this topic if you want to become an outstanding programmer.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob03aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "outline ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the scheme or map of something ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Before creating any object, you'd better create its class first as it's basically the outline that the object will have once it's finished.  ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob03aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "stem from ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to originate from", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(phrasal verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Now, you may want to know about the four different concepts that stem from OOP.  ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob03aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "relevant ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "extremely important ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: First, you will have to learn abstraction, in which the programmer only displays the most relevant information depending on the context.  ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob03aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "safety measure ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a preventive action taken to avoid any accident", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Now, you'd better bear in mind that encapsulation refers to a safety measure that protects data and restricts access to only authorized users.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob03aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "delimit ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to define something ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A method delimits how an object behaves.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n5le01ob03aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Look for extra information about object-oriented and classes' examples, read them carefully and take notes.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Describe each detail of one of them and create a presentation to explain their features.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share with your partners and answer their questions.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I look for extra information about object-oriented and classes' examples.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I create a presentation to explain their features.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I talk about object-oriented and classes' features and compere with basic examples.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Breaking Down Concepts in Object Oriented Programming", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "In the engineering field, there are <b>concepts in OOP</b> that you have to completely understand. Take a look at the following chart to learn more about the four basic concepts that belong to the <b>OOP</b> area.  ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":5,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"CONCEPT\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"DESCRIPTION\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Abstraction \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"This concept refers to the situation in which the programmer only displays the most important information depending on the context. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Encapsulation \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"This concepts refers to a safety measure programmers can use. It protects data and allows access to authorized users; furthermore, it hides internal mechanisms as well as data structures. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Inheritance \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Bear in mind that this concept is a process in which new classes emerge from pre-existing ones, keeping the general characteristics of these. It's a way to reuse codes. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Polymorphism \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"This concepts refers to the situation in which multiple forms share the same name. Plus, the characteristics that an object has can be modified according to the context. \\\"}\"],\"styles\":\"\"}", "type": "table"}]}]}